{"context":"ADR-023 defines the registry concept: metadata format, discovery, installation and update semantics, validation at adoption, and the offline/bundled install path. Ownership of distribution metadata rests with the Core registry concept, but the hosting model was explicitly delegated: \"hosting model is an ADR decision — ADR-030\" (§4.5). This ADR decides where registry metadata and standard content physically live. Source: ADR-030-registry-distribution-channel.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"- **Decentralized/static index bias.** Registry metadata lives in repositories; standard content follows the existing GitHub-releases pattern. There is no Core-operated registry service in v2.\n- **Registry is distribution metadata, not content hosting.** The registry indexes standard metadata (name, version, declared contract version, capability declaration, distribution location) and resolves content from the standard's own release channel.\n- **Integrity via trust validation.** Content integrity is enforced at install by ADR-022 trust validation (integrity verification, publisher attestation, pinning) — not by the distribution channel itself.\n- **Marketplace/community phase is not part of this decision.** Community enablement remains gated on Phase 7 outcomes; this ADR governs first-party","consequences":"**Positive:** no single point of failure for adoption; no Core-operated hosting burden; standard releases reuse existing release mechanics; the registry stays metadata, keeping distribution and trust separate concerns.\n\n**Negative:** discovery depends on index freshness; no marketplace features (search, curation) until the gated community phase; offline/bundled install requires the ADR-023 path; per-publisher release channels must be uniformly validated at adoption.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Core-operated registry service.** Rejected: creates the single point of failure §11.1 mitigates, requires Core-operated infrastructure for ecosystem content, and blurs \"registry is metadata, not content\".\n- **Central content mirror.** Rejected: content hosting is not the registry's role; the trust model (ADR-022) applies at install, so a mirror adds failure surface without adding integrity.\n- **Raw release channels without any index.** Rejected: loses discovery and validation at "}